{
  "case_id": "2005-04-944",
  "document_hash": "9385a9469a582af5fde7757949b64825df401bc4aac056760ff88ce24db98fe9",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Jeffrey A. Schwartz",
      "case_id": "2005-04-944",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9385a9469a582af5fde7757949b64825df401bc4aac056760ff88ce24db98fe9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9385a9469a58-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "A Federal court has subject matter jurisdiction over a claim brought under title VII regardless of whether an employer has 15 employees. This is so because when Congress enacted title VII, it included a specific jurisdictional grant and that grant provides that jurisdiction will exist in the Federal courts over all claims brought under the act. This specific grant of jurisdiction is consistent with the more general grant of jurisdiction contained in 28 U.S.C. 1331 wherein Federal question jurisdiction exists over all claims that arise under a Federal law. Title VII is a Federal law."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2005-04-944",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9385a9469a582af5fde7757949b64825df401bc4aac056760ff88ce24db98fe9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9385a9469a58-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: So you say -- so now it's essentially redundant. Now that 1331 has no jurisdictional amount, no amount in controversy, the jurisdictional provision in title VII is just going over the same territory. It doesn't add or detract. Is that so?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Daryl Joseffer",
      "case_id": "2005-04-944",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9385a9469a582af5fde7757949b64825df401bc4aac056760ff88ce24db98fe9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9385a9469a58-0000-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 18,
        "start": 18
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Justice O'Connor, we think the rule here, because it's a question of congressional intent, is simply how to best interpret the relevant statutes. Here, Congress twice unambiguously conferred jurisdiction over all title VII claims, at least twice: first in section 1331 and again in the title VII jurisdictional provision which confers jurisdiction over all claims brought under title VII."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2005-04-944",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9385a9469a582af5fde7757949b64825df401bc4aac056760ff88ce24db98fe9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9385a9469a58-0001-0001",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I agree there are three practical consequences, and from that perspective, it makes little sense to believe that Congress would have thought that this requirement should be jurisdictional."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2005-04-944",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9385a9469a582af5fde7757949b64825df401bc4aac056760ff88ce24db98fe9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9385a9469a58-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: I'm sorry. What have juries been determining?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2005-04-944",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9385a9469a582af5fde7757949b64825df401bc4aac056760ff88ce24db98fe9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9385a9469a58-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 31,
        "start": 31
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: What other aspects of title VII do you think are jurisdictional?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice O'Connor",
      "case_id": "2005-04-944",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9385a9469a582af5fde7757949b64825df401bc4aac056760ff88ce24db98fe9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9385a9469a58-0001-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE O'CONNOR: But the practical consequences of affirming here are severe in terms of handling these cases."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2005-04-944",
      "confidence": 0.98,
      "document_hash": "9385a9469a582af5fde7757949b64825df401bc4aac056760ff88ce24db98fe9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9385a9469a58-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: May I go back to Zipes?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2005-04-944",
      "confidence": 0.97,
      "document_hash": "9385a9469a582af5fde7757949b64825df401bc4aac056760ff88ce24db98fe9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9385a9469a58-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
